course: dictionary
lesson: 10163
title: A word for an animal we may have lost
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A thylacine is an animal that once lived in Australia and on the island of Tasmania. Its other name, the Tasmanian tiger, is a clue to what it looked like: it had stripes on its back. The sentence «The thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, was a striped meat-eating marsupial» tells you that the two names mean the same animal.
explain: Look at the words around thylacine to work out the rest. A marsupial is an animal whose babies grow in a pouch. The sentence says the thylacine was «shaped like a wolf», so it looked like a dog, not a tiger. Always check your idea: could you swap in the name of another animal and still make sense?
explain: The word «last» is an important clue. «The last known one died in Hobart Zoo in 1936» means there were no more known thylacines after that. Scientists believe the thylacine has died out, so it is now extinct. When we say an animal is extinct, it means no living ones are left.
fact: A thylacine is also called a Tasmanian tiger or Tasmanian wolf.
fact: A marsupial is an animal whose babies grow in a pouch.
fact: The thylacine lived in Australia and Tasmania, and it is believed to have died out.
fact: The last known thylacine died in a zoo in Hobart nearly a hundred years ago, in 1936.
fact: People still report sightings of thylacines, but none have been proven.
exercise: «The thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, was a striped meat-eating marsupial.» What is another name for a thylacine? | *Tasmanian tiger | Kangaroo | Platypus | Sabre-toothed cat
exercise: What does marsupial mean? | A plant that grows in water | A tiger from India | An animal with no legs | *An animal whose babies grow in a pouch
exercise: «People still report thylacine sightings, but none have been proven.» What does sightings mean? | Hunts for food in the forest | Old bones dug up from the ground | Paintings in a museum | *Times when someone says they saw the animal
test: «Despite many reported sightings, there has never been clear proof any survived.» What does proof mean? | A kind of cloth | A sad feeling | A loud noise | *Clear evidence that something is true
test: Why were farmers paid to kill thylacines? | It was the rules of the zoo | They wanted to keep it as a pet | They were looking for a new home for it | *They blamed it for killing sheep
exam: Why is the thylacine a sad example in science lessons? | It is the biggest animal in Australia | *It shows an animal that became extinct because of hunting, habitat loss and disease | It can fly faster than any bird | It shows how to build a good zoo
